          "reasoning": "**Ensuring error handling clarity**\n\nI'm focusing on adjusting the code while keeping the existing messages and HTTP status unchanged. I need to add a `request_id` field for specific error pathways, ensuring it's only included in error responses and not success ones. The plan is to return this ID in the response JSON when an error occurs, while also providing acceptance tests to check that everything works as intended. I must ensure the `request_id` doesn't include any sensitive user information for security reasons.**Clarifying error handling**\n\nI need to keep the code, message, and status code unchanged for error detection. So, I don't want to create new detection criteria. The instructions should specify that the frontend will extract and display the `request_id` only if an error response contains it and is one of the two specified error types. If the `request_id` is missing, it shouldn't be shown. The UI should present the `request_id` as a non-sensitive string and ensure it isn't logged in persistent storage, which is a bit tricky with analytics.**Finalizing request_id handling**\n\nI need to make sure that the `request_id` isn't treated as a credential and isn't included in any further requests. I'll set up acceptance tests that simulate error responses with the `request_id` present to verify that the UI displays it correctly, while ensuring success responses don’t show it. I want to avoid logging the `request_id` in ways that could leak it, but it can be displayed for user feedback. I'll craft a JSON with precise properties and include necessary instructions clearly.",
